# STAGE 8 — RESTORE THE CRYPTOGRAPHIC AUDIT ENGINE

The previous source audit found fabricated hash strings rather than genuine SHA-256 verification.

Implement actual digest calculation and chain verification.

## 8.1 Canonical serialization

Define one deterministic canonical representation for each integrity-relevant event.

Include the fields necessary to detect unauthorized changes to work agreements, recorded hours, wage calculations, payments, acknowledgments, corrections and disputes.

The representation must be stable, versioned and independent of UI formatting.

Keep one authoritative serialization and hashing implementation.

Use the actual Web Crypto SHA-256 implementation or another correctly implemented standard SHA-256 implementation. Do not generate fake hashes or random hash-looking strings.

Since Web Crypto digest operations are asynchronous, use an asynchronous design unless an actual tested implementation requires otherwise.

## 8.2 Audit history

Every relevant correction or event must be appended as a new event with stable identifiers and appropriate prior-event references.

Verify the digest, previous-hash linkage, expected sequence and history completeness independently.

Where concurrent remote writes could create competing sequence numbers, use an appropriate transactional backend mechanism or a design that avoids falsely claiming a globally ordered chain.

Never trust a client-supplied digest without recomputing it.

Distinguish client-recorded time from server-received time.

## 8.3 Required outcomes

Implement and test these distinct outcomes:

1. Integrity check passed for the records examined.
2. Record modification detected.
3. Previous history incomplete or linkage broken.
4. Verification could not be completed.

An individual digest passing does not imply that the entire chain is valid.

A locally controlled hash chain cannot guarantee protection against an attacker who can rewrite all records and recompute all hashes. Explain this honestly.

Use trusted external checkpoints or independent signatures only if actually implemented.

